    "case_id": "amb1-case05-family-archive",
    "focal_region": {
      "annotated_sentence_text": "[Turn 1 USER]\ne001\tMy brother Tomas and I have been digitizing boxes of family photographs, letters, and recordings.\ne002\tA local cultural center offered us a small exhibition next spring about migration into the neighborhood.\ne003\tTomas wants to accept because a public date would help us finish and the material could mean something beyond our family.\ne004\tI want the archive organized first.\ne005\tRight now captions are incomplete, some stories conflict, and I worry the exhibition will turn a complicated family history into a clean public narrative.\n[Turn 1 ASSISTANT]\ne006\tThe exhibition could create momentum and public value, but it also changes the purpose of the archive from preservation to interpretation.\ne007\tYou may not need to finish the whole archive before exhibiting a carefully bounded selection.\ne008\tThe first question is whether you and Tomas agree on what the exhibition is allowed to claim, especially where accounts conflict.\ne009\tA date can help with completion while also pressuring you to resolve ambiguity cosmetically.\n[Turn 2 USER]\ne010\tI do not think Tomas wants to resolve it cosmetically.\ne011\tHe is usually the one who includes awkward stories.\ne012\tI am the person who keeps building the catalog and saying we need better dates and names.\ne013\tHe thinks my standard of organization is a way of postponing anything public until nobody is interested.\ne014\tThat hurts because there is some truth in it.\ne015\tBut when he tells stories confidently, relatives often remember them differently.\n[Turn 2 ASSISTANT]\ne016\tThen the difference may be less honesty versus polish and more tolerance for incomplete evidence.\ne017\tYour cataloging protects traceability; his storytelling keeps the material connected to living attention.\ne018\tAn exhibition could show conflicting memories rather than choose among them, although that approach can become a curatorial device that feels cleverer than the family experience.\ne019\tIt would help to identify which disagreements are enriching and which could make someone feel misrepresented.\n[Turn 3 USER]\ne020\tOur aunt Leena is enthusiastic and has already offered to record narration.\ne021\tOur cousin Mara does not want a letter from her father displayed, even though Tomas says it explains why that branch of the family left.\ne022\tI agree with Mara about the letter.\ne023\tTomas says one person's discomfort should not control a shared history, but he has not said he would use it without permission.\ne024\tI catch myself treating his argument as a threat when it may be him thinking aloud.\n[Turn 3 ASSISTANT]\ne025\tIt is useful to separate the principle he is testing from the action he intends.\ne026\tMara's objection is specific and should be treated as real, while the broader question of who can speak for shared material remains unresolved.\ne027\tYou could exclude that letter and still acknowledge the missing part of the story, but doing so may shift the narrative in ways other relatives contest.\ne028\tConsent decisions do not automatically produce a neutral history; they define what kind of public project you are willing to make.",
